{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"182e5d0f\" lines=\"89-103\">\n<description>\nMove each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.\n- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.\n\nRule:\n- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.\n- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.\n- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3bd292e8\" lines=\"13-29\">\n<description>\nRecolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\nRule\n- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n  grey and green across every wall adjacency.\nKey insight\n- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"30f42897\" lines=\"51-66\">\n<description>\nRepeat the single colored border segment around the rectangular border, leaving one segment-length gap between copies.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There is exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous segment on the rectangular border, possibly crossing the cycle start.\n\nRule:\n- Unwrap the grid border clockwise into a cyclic path.\n- Let n be the length of the foreground segment.\n- Copy the segment with the same color every 2*n border positions around the cycle.\n\nKey insight:\n- The foreground segment is not extended by rows or columns directly; it is a motif on the perimeter cycle, repeated with an equal-sized gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\" lines=\"32-46\">\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b9c3697\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nInsert red components into interior sockets of large structures, leaving a black trail.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common. Large non-bg, non-red structures with interior cavities.\n- Red components outside structures.\n\nRule:\n- Interior sockets: bg cells inside a structure's bbox, adjacent to structure pixels.\n- For each structure, find the direction where rays from sockets hit a red component.\n- Slide that component into the socket; paint traversed cells black.\n- Unmatched reds vanish.\n\nKey insight: Docking direction is inferred by ray-casting from interior sockets to the nearest red component.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5d588b4d\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.\n- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.\n\nRule:\n- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths\n  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.\n- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.\n- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row\n  with background pixels.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length\ndiamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6a980be1\" lines=\"46-61\">\n<description>\nProject the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.\n\nInput structure\n- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.\n- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.\n- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.\n\nRule\n- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.\n- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.\n- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.\n\nKey insight\n- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"252143c9\" lines=\"14-33\">\n<description>\nKeep only the center color and redraw it as a diagonal ray to its indicated corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an odd square with a unique dominant background color.\n- The center pixel is foreground.\n- Every off-center pixel with the center color lies in the same diagonal\n  quadrant relative to the center.\n\nRule:\n- Fill the whole output with the background color.\n- Use the quadrant of the center-colored pixels to choose one diagonal\n  direction from the center.\n- Draw a continuous line of the center color from the center to the grid\n  edge in that direction.\n\nKey insight:\nThe color at the exact center identifies the surviving color, while the\nother pixels of that color identify which corner the center should connect to.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b8318e3\" lines=\"86-105\">\n<description>\nMove each isolated colored pixel into the one-cell frame around a grey 2x2 block.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background is the dominant color.\n- Grey foreground objects are solid 2x2 anchor blocks.\n- Every other non-background, non-grey cell is a colored singleton source.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the grey anchors fixed and clear all colored singleton source pixels.\n- For each singleton and each anchor, the normal destination is the cell reached by\n  clamping the pixel into the anchor's one-cell outer frame.\n- If a diagonally outside singleton has an exact 8-direction sightline into an anchor,\n  also allow the cell just before the first grey pixel on that ray.\n- Choose distinct destinations that minimize total king-move distance; at equal\n  distance prefer exact sightlines, then the shorter Manhattan movement.\n\nKey insight: The grey 2x2 blocks define magnetic frames; conflicts are resolved\nstructurally by nearest-frame assignment rather than by color identity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aa62e3f4\" lines=\"18-35\">\n<description>\nReplace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.\n- The outline is the unique largest foreground color.\n- One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color\n  is the unique smallest non-outline color.\n\nRule:\n- Erase all original foreground pixels to background.\n- Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the\n  marker color.\n- Keep the grid shape unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the\nlargest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9968a131\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nShift alternating two-color row bars so each row aligns to the topmost bar's color columns.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background surrounds horizontal two-pixel bars.\n- Every foreground row has exactly one adjacent bar using the same two colors.\n- The topmost bar gives the reference left/right column for each color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep bars whose left color is already in its reference column.\n- For a reversed bar, shift the whole two-pixel bar horizontally until its\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
